WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.600
She came to inventory an archive. The archive was expecting her.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:17.730
Miranda Cale is hired to catalogue a missing scientist's estate.
He has left behind sealed experiments, transcripts, and a
question.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:27.010
Eleven doors on the county plan. Twelve in her inventory. Then a
document begins to describe her work before she has done it.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:39.930
Three isolated conversations. The same interruption. Is the
archive preserving a life, or assembling something from the people
who read it?

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:49.890
When a voice is reconstructed from a person's words, what
survives? The person, a fiction, or something we have no name for?

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.940
And if a voice can answer, can it refuse? Who gets to decide when
the conversation ends?

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:09.310
A novel of archival horror, about keeping, consent, and the right
to leave. The narrative is fiction. The estate is not.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:22.850
Built from a real public archive. Intended to be held in print.
Available to read digitally for free. Purchasing supports the
continuing Companion Dossier.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:29.500
Using This Matter. A Novel of the Companion Estate. By Jacob E.
Thomas. Enter the archive.

